---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Leaderboard">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/tabulator-tables@6.3.0/dist/css/tabulator_simple.min.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <h1>Oscillation Leaderboard — +10% harvests per year</h1>
  <p>
    The goal is simple compounding: repeated <strong>+10% harvests</strong> on stocks that
    oscillate a lot while net-trending up. Every completed <strong>+10% leg</strong>
    (anchor-reset rule) is one harvestable recovery, so the ranking key is
    <strong>positive oscillations per year</strong> among tickers whose lookback drift is
    positive — downtrenders are kept but greyed and ranked last. We want oscillators, not
    parabolic runners: a ticker that spiked 3x+ from its trailing 12-month low (🚀) printed
    its legs in a one-way regime shift, so it is greyed and ranked below every steady
    candidate. Consecutive down-legs are
    never penalized: a stock that dipped three times before recovering is oscillation
    richness, not failure. <strong>Signal</strong> is simply the direction of the latest
    ±10% event (down leg = <code>BUY</code>, up leg = <code>SELL</code>); whether to act
    is your decision — nothing here tracks trades. Investigate any ticker in the chart
    explorer.
  </p>

  <p id="bullish-meta"><em>Loading screen…</em></p>

  <div id="bullish-scatter" style="min-height:520px;"></div>

  <h2>Leaderboard</h2>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
    Don't see a stock?
    <a href="https://github.com/sivadotblog/stock-threshold-analyzer/issues/new?template=add-ticker.md&title=Add+ticker%3A+XXXX&labels=add-ticker"
       target="_blank" rel="noopener"
       style="font-weight:600;">Open a GitHub issue to request it →</a>
  </p>

  <div id="tb-filters" class="tb-filter-bar">
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-ticker">Ticker</label>
      <input type="text" id="f-ticker" placeholder="e.g. RKLB" autocomplete="off" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-rank-max">Rank ≤</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-rank-max" placeholder="e.g. 100" min="1" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-osc-min">Up-legs/yr ≥</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-osc-min" placeholder="e.g. 8" step="0.5" min="0" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label>CAGR%</label>
      <div class="tb-filter-range">
        <input type="number" id="f-cagr-min" placeholder="min" />
        <span>–</span>
        <input type="number" id="f-cagr-max" placeholder="max" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group tb-filter-group--btn">
      <button id="f-reset">Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="bullish-table"></div>

  <div class="field-legend">
    <h2>Field Descriptions</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Last signal / Prev</strong></td>
          <td>The two most recent ±10% threshold events — direction, price, and date.
          These are <em>history, not realtime states</em>: <code>BUY @ 100 · 6/10</code>
          means a −10% leg completed at $100 on June 10 and nothing has triggered since.
          <code>SELL</code>: a +10% leg (harvest) completed. Note a SELL is anchor-based,
          not entry-based: if interim dips reset the anchor lower, a SELL can fire below
          <em>your</em> entry +10% — judge it against your own position.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Target</strong></td>
          <td>Where the opposite signal fires from the last event's price. After a
          <code>BUY @ 100</code> the harvest is <code>SELL ≥ 110</code>; after a
          <code>SELL @ 110</code> the next dip trigger is <code>BUY ≤ 99</code>.
          (Another −10% leg below a BUY also resets the anchor — and the target — lower.)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Up-legs/yr</strong></td>
          <td>The ranking key: completed +10% legs per year of data. Each up-leg is one
          realized, harvestable +10% recovery — no pairing with dips, no outcome
          classification, so overlapping dip clusters can't distort it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>n▲ / n▼</strong></td>
          <td>Raw counts of +10% and −10% legs over the window — the evidence behind the
          rate, never hidden. A young listing can show a high rate on few legs; check n▲.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Since%</strong></td>
          <td>How far price has moved since the signal event's close. On a BUY row, a
          negative value means the dip has deepened further below the trigger.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>CAGR%</strong></td>
          <td>Growth context: annualized return over the lookback. Tickers with negative
          drift (📉, greyed) are not candidates no matter how much they oscillate — they
          rank below every trend-positive name.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MaxDD%</strong></td>
          <td>Volatility context: worst peak-to-trough drawdown over the lookback. The
          price of admission for the oscillations.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1y run↑%</strong></td>
          <td>The parabolic gate: the largest gain from the lowest close in any trailing
          12-month window over the lookback — daily closes vs. the trailing low, no
          averaging. QQQ/SPY sit around +40%; CIFR's $3 → $25 spike is +700%. At
          <strong>+200% (3x) or more</strong> the ticker is flagged 🚀, greyed, and ranked
          below every steady trend-positive name: those ±10% legs came from a one-way
          regime shift, not a repeatable dip-cycle, so the oscillation history can't be
          trusted to repeat. The threshold is configurable
          (<code>analyzer.parabolic_max_run_up_pct</code>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Streak</strong></td>
          <td>Current momentum. How many consecutive ±10% threshold crossings have gone the same direction. <code>+3↑</code> = the last 3 events were all up legs. <code>-2↓</code> = last 2 were down legs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Recent Δ</strong></td>
          <td>Every ±10% threshold crossing in the last 30 days, oldest to newest — e.g. <code>-1↓ -1↓ +1↑</code> for two down-legs then an up-leg. Based on the actual sequence of events, not calendar days, so it isn't affected by weekends/holidays and naturally shows more badges for an active stock and fewer for a quiet one.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="https://unpkg.com/tabulator-tables@6.3.0/dist/js/tabulator.min.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src={`${base}/js/bullish-screen.js`}></script>
</Base>
